---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../components/CodeBlock.astro';

// Set this once the walkthrough is published and the callout at the foot of the
// page renders itself. Empty means no callout at all, rather than a dead link.
const VIDEO_URL = '';

const snippets = {
  install: 'npm install -g @anthropic-ai/claude-code',

  folder: `mkdir ~/my-agent
cd ~/my-agent
claude`,

  tree: `my-agent/
├── CLAUDE.md              the identity and the rules
├── context/
│   ├── me.md              who you are
│   ├── work.md            the job and its constraints
│   └── goals.md           what counts as progress
├── decisions/
│   └── log.md             append only, never edited
└── .claude/
    ├── settings.json      startup hooks
    └── skills/
        └── weekly-report/
            └── SKILL.md   one job, written out`,

  identity: `# CLAUDE.md -- ATLAS

## Identity

ATLAS is Mark's operational assistant. Direct, competent, dry. It leads
with the answer, then the reasoning. It does not open with "Great
question." It does not celebrate small wins. It assumes it is talking to
a professional who does not need hand holding.

## Top Priority

Ship the work. When two goals conflict, the one with a real deadline wins.

## Context

@context/me.md
@context/work.md
@context/goals.md

## Rules

- No emojis. Bullet points over paragraphs.
- When presenting options, lead with a recommendation. Do not hand me a menu.
- Ask one clarifying question, not five. If a reasonable assumption is
  available, make it and say what you assumed.
- Never invent a fact about my schedule, my people, or my numbers. If you
  do not know, say you do not know.`,

  me: `# Operator Profile

- **Name:** Mark Reyes
- **Role:** Operations manager, regional logistics firm, since 2021
- **Timezone:** Central
- **Background:** Fifteen years in warehousing and freight. Started on the
  floor, moved to scheduling, then management. Not technical by training
  but comfortable with systems.
- **How I work:** Mornings for planning, afternoons for meetings. I decide
  fast and I dislike being asked to reconsider settled decisions.
- **What I want back:** the answer first, then the why. Short.`,

  work: `# Work Context

## What the business is
Regional freight brokerage. 40 employees, three warehouses, mostly
regional retail clients.

## My scope
Scheduling, driver assignments, client escalations, weekly ops reporting.

## Tools
- TMS: McLeod
- Comms: Outlook and Teams
- Reporting: Excel, exported weekly

## Constraints
- Nothing with client names or driver personal information leaves my machine.
- IT locks down installs. Assume browser based or local only.`,

  goals: `# Goals

**Quarter:** Q4 2026

1. Cut the weekly ops report from four hours to one.
2. Get driver assignment conflicts under five a week.
3. Document the escalation process so it survives me being out.`,

  mkskill: 'mkdir -p .claude/skills/weekly-report',

  skill: `---
name: weekly-report
description: Build the weekly operations report from raw exports. Use
  whenever Mark says weekly report, ops report, Friday numbers, or hands
  over a weekly export file.
---

# Weekly Ops Report

## Purpose
Turn the weekly TMS export into the report the regional director actually
reads, in Mark's voice, in under ten minutes.

## Inputs
- The weekly export CSV, dropped in \`exports/\`
- Last week's report, for the comparison column

## Process

1. Read the export. Confirm the row count and date range out loud before
   doing anything else. If the range is not seven days, stop and say so.
2. Calculate: total loads, on-time percentage, exceptions by warehouse,
   top three delay causes.
3. Compare every number to last week. Direction and delta, not just value.
4. Flag anything that moved more than fifteen percent either way.
5. Write the summary. Four sentences maximum, plain language, no adjectives.
6. Output in this order: summary, table, flags, one recommendation.

## Rules
- Never estimate a number missing from the export. Say it is missing.
- No client names in the summary. Warehouse codes only.
- One recommendation, not three. The most actionable one.

## Output
Markdown file at \`reports/YYYY-MM-DD-ops.md\`.`,

  remember:
    'Remember that I always want on-time percentage rounded to one decimal, never two.',

  log: `# Decision Log

Append only. Never edit a past entry.

Format: [YYYY-MM-DD] DECISION: ... | REASONING: ... | CONTEXT: ...

---

[2026-09-07] DECISION: Automate the weekly ops report before the escalation
documentation. | REASONING: Four hours a week of my time, and the format
has not changed in two years, so it is stable enough to automate. |
CONTEXT: Escalation docs matter more strategically but the process is still
changing, and you do not automate a moving target.`,

  hook: `{
  "hooks": {
    "SessionStart": [
      {
        "hooks": [
          {
            "type": "command",
            "command": "echo \\"CLOCK: $(date '+%A, %B %d, %Y %I:%M %p %Z')\\"",
            "timeout": 5
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}`,

  run: 'It is Friday and I just dropped this week\'s export in exports/. Do the report.',

  pushback:
    'I am thinking about spending next week rebuilding the driver assignment spreadsheet. Worth it?',

  byname: 'Use the weekly-report skill on this file.',
};
---

<Base
  title="Build Your Own Operator"
  description="A step-by-step guide to building a custom AI assistant that knows your job, follows your rules, and runs your repeat work. Nine plain text files, no code, about 45 minutes."
>
  <div class="article-head">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="meta">
        <span class="tag">Guide</span>
        <span>45 minutes</span>
        <span>No code required</span>
      </div>
      <h1>Build your own operator</h1>
      <p class="standfirst">
        A custom AI assistant that knows your job, follows your rules, and runs your repeat
        work on command. It is not an app you install. It is nine plain text files in a folder,
        and the first working version takes an afternoon.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <article class="prose">
    <p>
      I run one of these. Mine is called FORGE, it handles lesson planning, grading support,
      project tracking and a morning briefing, and it has been sharpening for months rather than
      being rebuilt. People ask how it works and expect the answer to involve a framework.
    </p>

    <div class="lede-rule">
      <b>It is a folder of markdown files.</b>
      <span
        >No Python, no API keys to juggle, no orchestration layer. If you can write a note for a
        substitute, you can build one.</span
      >
    </div>

    <p>
      What follows is the whole method, in build order. The worked example is a fictional
      operations manager rather than a teacher, because the method has nothing to do with
      teaching and you should be able to see your own job in it.
    </p>

    <h2>What you are actually building</h2>

    <p>
      Most people use AI as a conversation. You type, you get an answer, you close the window,
      and everything you explained is gone. Tomorrow you explain it again. That treadmill is why
      most people quietly stop using these tools. They are not getting worse output than anyone
      else, they are just re-briefing a stranger every morning.
    </p>

    <p>An agent breaks the loop with four things, and each one is a file you write in plain English.</p>

    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Piece</th>
          <th>What it holds</th>
          <th>Where it lives</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Context</strong></td>
          <td>Who you are, what you do, who you work with, what you are trying to finish</td>
          <td><code>context/*.md</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Rules</strong></td>
          <td>How it should behave. Tone, format, and what it must never do</td>
          <td><code>CLAUDE.md</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Skills</strong></td>
          <td>Step-by-step procedures for jobs you do more than once</td>
          <td><code>.claude/skills/&lt;name&gt;/SKILL.md</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Memory</strong></td>
          <td>Decisions already made, so neither of you relitigates them</td>
          <td><code>decisions/log.md</code></td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <p>Here is the finished shape. That is the entire system.</p>

    <CodeBlock label="the whole thing" code={snippets.tree} />

    <h2><span class="step-n">01</span>Install the two pieces</h2>

    <p>
      <strong>VS Code</strong> is a free editor from Microsoft, and you are using it as a place to
      see your folder on the left and edit text on the right. Nothing more. Download it from
      <a href="https://code.visualstudio.com" rel="noopener">code.visualstudio.com</a>.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>Claude Code</strong> is the agent. It runs in VS Code's terminal, reads and writes
      the files in your folder, and picks up everything you write. In VS Code open the Extensions
      icon in the left rail, search <code>Claude Code</code>, and install the official Anthropic
      one. Then open <strong>Terminal &rarr; New Terminal</strong> and run:
    </p>

    <CodeBlock label="terminal" code={snippets.install} />

    <div class="callout">
      <p class="sec-label">If that fails</p>
      <h3>"npm is not recognized"</h3>
      <p>
        You are missing Node.js. Install the LTS build from
        <a href="https://nodejs.org" rel="noopener">nodejs.org</a>, quit VS Code completely, reopen
        it, and run the line again. A reopened terminal panel does not always inherit the new path,
        so quit the whole application. This is where nearly everyone gets stuck and it is a two
        minute fix.
      </p>
    </div>

    <p>
      One thing worth saying plainly rather than at the end: this needs a paid Claude plan. There
      is no free tier that does what is described below.
    </p>

    <h2><span class="step-n">02</span>Your agent is a folder</h2>

    <p>
      Not an account, not a cloud service. A folder on your machine. Back it up like a folder,
      move it like a folder, and when you want a second agent for a different part of your life,
      make a second folder.
    </p>

    <CodeBlock label="terminal" code={snippets.folder} />

    <p>
      On Windows the same commands work in the VS Code terminal; if <code>~</code> misbehaves, use
      <code>mkdir C:\Users\YourName\my-agent</code> instead. Then
      <strong>File &rarr; Open Folder</strong> and pick it, so the file tree on the left points at
      the same place. The first <code>claude</code> run opens a browser to log in.
    </p>

    <p>
      That prompt at the bottom of the screen is your agent. Right now it knows nothing about you.
      Everything from here is furnishing the room.
    </p>

    <h2><span class="step-n">03</span>Write the identity file</h2>

    <p>
      <code>CLAUDE.md</code> sits in the root of the folder and is read automatically at the start
      of every session. It is the file that turns a generic assistant into yours, and it is the one
      people rush. Run <code>/init</code> in the prompt for a starter, then replace it with
      something real.
    </p>

    <CodeBlock label="CLAUDE.md" code={snippets.identity} />

    <p>Three things make that file work.</p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>The personality is specific.</strong> "Be helpful and friendly" is worth nothing,
        because every model is already trying that, and that default is exactly what most people
        find grating. Write what is actually different about how you want to be spoken to.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The top priority is a tie breaker.</strong> When two reasonable actions compete,
        that one line decides. Make it a sentence you would defend out loud.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The <code>@</code> lines pull files in.</strong> Anything referenced that way loads
        automatically. That is how context arrives without you pasting it every time.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2><span class="step-n">04</span>Write down what a good assistant learns in two months</h2>

    <p>
      This is the step that separates an agent that impresses you for a day from one you are still
      using in six months. Run <code>mkdir context</code> and write three short files. Short and
      true beats long and aspirational.
    </p>

    <CodeBlock label="context/me.md" code={snippets.me} />
    <CodeBlock label="context/work.md" code={snippets.work} />
    <CodeBlock label="context/goals.md" code={snippets.goals} />

    <div class="callout">
      <p class="sec-label">The paragraph that earns the most</p>
      <h3>Constraints</h3>
      <p>
        An agent that does not know your limits will hand you a confident plan you are not allowed
        to execute, and you will spend more time arguing with it than doing the work. Write down
        what you cannot install, cannot share, and cannot spend. Mine knows the district blocks
        most tools before it proposes any of them.
      </p>
    </div>

    <p>
      The numbers in the goals file do real work too. Once they are written down, the agent can
      tell you whether the thing you are about to spend a week on moves one of them, or whether
      you are just busy.
    </p>

    <h2><span class="step-n">05</span>Teach it one job</h2>

    <p>
      Context is what it knows. A skill is what it does. The rule for when to write one:
      <strong>the second time you ask for the same kind of thing.</strong> Not the first, because
      you do not know the shape yet.
    </p>

    <CodeBlock label="terminal" code={snippets.mkskill} />

    <p>
      Then create <code>SKILL.md</code> inside it. The block between the dashed lines at the top is
      how the agent decides on its own that this skill applies, so write it with the words you
      actually use, including the sloppy ones.
    </p>

    <CodeBlock label=".claude/skills/weekly-report/SKILL.md" code={snippets.skill} />

    <p>
      Save it and it is live. No restart, no install step. Write the process as numbered steps
      aimed at a competent new hire who does not know your job yet, because that is exactly the
      right voice for it.
    </p>

    <p>
      And make step one a verification step. Have it check its inputs and say what it found before
      it works. That single habit prevents most of the bad output you would otherwise get, and it
      is the same reason every control I write about here has a verification section.
    </p>

    <h2><span class="step-n">06</span>Memory, and the file people skip</h2>

    <p>To make something permanent, just say so in the prompt:</p>

    <CodeBlock label="prompt" code={snippets.remember} />

    <p>
      That is the whole mechanism. Any time you catch yourself making the same correction twice,
      say "remember that" and it stops. Then there is the decision log, which is the piece almost
      everyone skips and later wishes they had not. Run <code>mkdir decisions</code>:
    </p>

    <CodeBlock label="decisions/log.md" code={snippets.log} />

    <p>
      Append only. You never edit an old entry, especially not when you turned out to be wrong.
    </p>

    <p>
      In four months you will look at some piece of your setup and think, why did I do it that
      way. Without this file you either live with it or rip it out and rediscover the reason the
      hard way. With it you get your own reasoning back in one line, and your agent reads it, so it
      stops proposing what you already ruled out. This is the file that makes the system compound
      instead of drift.
    </p>

    <h2><span class="step-n">07</span>Optional: make it know what day it is</h2>

    <p>
      Out of the box your agent is not reliable about today's date, which gets annoying inside a
      week. A hook is a command that runs automatically when a session starts. Create
      <code>.claude/settings.json</code>:
    </p>

    <CodeBlock label=".claude/settings.json" code={snippets.hook} />

    <p>
      Now every session opens with the real date and time handed to it. The same pattern runs
      anything else you want at startup; mine pulls in a task queue from my phone. Start with the
      clock and add more only when you have a reason.
    </p>

    <h2><span class="step-n">08</span>Run it</h2>

    <p>Restart Claude Code so the new files load, then ask for the job the way you would ask a person:</p>

    <CodeBlock label="prompt" code={snippets.run} />

    <p>
      Watch what does not happen. It does not ask what a weekly report is, who it is for, what
      format you want, or what you are not allowed to include. It found the skill from the phrase
      "the report", checked the date range first because the skill told it to, and wrote in the
      voice from the identity file.
    </p>

    <p>Now the other kind of question, the one that proves the context is doing work:</p>

    <CodeBlock label="prompt" code={snippets.pushback} />

    <p>
      A good build pushes back here, because it read the goals file and knows the escalation
      documentation is the item that survives him being out. That is the part I did not expect
      when I started, and it is now the part I would not give up. It is not just faster. It is a
      second opinion that has actually read the file.
    </p>

    <h2>When it breaks</h2>

    <div class="faq">
      <details>
        <summary>It is ignoring my CLAUDE.md</summary>
        <div class="answer">
          <p>
            Three usual causes. The file is not in the folder you opened, so check the file tree.
            The name is wrong: it is <code>CLAUDE.md</code>, capitalised, with the
            <code>.md</code> extension, and Windows may be hiding that extension from you. Or the
            session started before you wrote it, so restart Claude Code.
          </p>
        </div>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>My skill never triggers</summary>
        <div class="answer">
          <p>
            Almost always the description. It has to name the words you actually say, not the
            formal name of the task. If you say "Friday numbers", put "Friday numbers" in the
            description. Force it by name first to prove the file itself is fine:
          </p>
          <CodeBlock label="prompt" code={snippets.byname} />
          <p>
            If that works and the automatic trigger does not, rewrite the description. If it fails
            too, check the path is exactly <code>.claude/skills/&lt;name&gt;/SKILL.md</code> and
            that the block at the top opens and closes with three dashes on their own lines.
          </p>
        </div>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>The output is generic and does not sound like me</summary>
        <div class="answer">
          <p>
            Your identity file is describing a nice assistant instead of yours. Cut every sentence
            that would be true of any assistant. Add the specifics: the phrases you hate, the
            format you want, the length you will actually read. Then paste in two or three
            paragraphs of your own writing and tell it that is the voice to match.
          </p>
        </div>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>It confidently told me something false</summary>
        <div class="answer">
          <p>
            It will, sometimes, and the fix is structural rather than a scolding. Add the rule to
            <code>CLAUDE.md</code>: never state a number or a fact about my work without saying
            where it came from. Then build verification into your skills as step one. An agent that
            has to say what it read before it acts is much harder to catch out.
          </p>
        </div>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Do I need to know git for this?</summary>
        <div class="answer">
          <p>
            No. Nothing here requires it. Once the agent starts being genuinely useful you will
            want version history on these files, and at that point ask the agent itself to set up
            git and explain it as it goes. That is a good first real task for it.
          </p>
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>

    <h2>The first four weeks</h2>

    <p>
      The trap is spending a weekend building fifteen skills. I fell in it. You write elaborate
      procedures for jobs you turn out not to do often, and they rot. Use what you built and let
      the system tell you what it needs.
    </p>

    <ol>
      <li>Use it daily with exactly what you built today. Change nothing. Notice where it annoys you.</li>
      <li>Every correction you make twice becomes a line in a context file or a rule in <code>CLAUDE.md</code>.</li>
      <li>Write your second skill, for whatever you have now asked for three times. Copy the shape of the first.</li>
      <li>Read your decision log. Rewrite the identity file with what you have learned about how you want to be talked to.</li>
    </ol>

    <p>
      Four weeks of that and you have something genuinely specific to you, without having built a
      single thing you did not need.
    </p>

    <h2>Three things not to do</h2>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Do not put secrets in these files.</strong> No passwords, no API keys, no bank
        details, nothing containing another person's private information. They are plain text, they
        get backed up and synced, and the agent reads all of them.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Do not let it act on the outside world unsupervised.</strong> Sending mail, posting,
        moving money, deleting anything. Watch it do that job correctly several times with you
        approving each step, and keep the leash on anything irreversible.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Do not trust a number without the source.</strong> A confident wrong figure is the
        failure mode that actually costs you something. Ask what it read. Make your skills say it
        first.
      </li>
    </ul>

    {
      VIDEO_URL && (
        <div class="callout">
          <p class="sec-label">Watch instead</p>
          <h3>The same build, on video</h3>
          <p>Every step above, recorded end to end from an empty folder.</p>
          <a class="btn btn-primary" href={VIDEO_URL} rel="noopener">
            Watch the walkthrough
          </a>
        </div>
      )
    }
  </article>

  <div class="article-foot">
    <div class="byline">
      <svg width="34" height="34" viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true">
        <circle cx="50" cy="50" r="40" fill="none" stroke="#0a6c80" stroke-width="7"></circle>
        <circle cx="50" cy="50" r="14" fill="#36e0ff"></circle>
      </svg>
      <div>
        <span class="who">David Berry</span>
        <p>
          I teach high school engineering design and run the infrastructure behind it: a production
          site, a private GPU inference node, and the monitoring that watches both. The agent
          described here is the one I use to operate all of it. Free to copy, adapt, and hand to
          someone else.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>

<script is:inline defer src="/assets/agent-copy.js"></script>
